  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule I am reading says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" The two reply texts are, in full, Reply A: '```text\\n{\"a\":1}\\n```\\n{\"a\":2}' and Reply B: '{\"a\":2}'. Under the rule, Reply A contains a code fence (the ```text fence) holding the object {\"a\":1}, so the rule requires the check to return {\"a\":1} for Reply A; Reply B has no fence, so the rule requires the check to return its last top-level object, {\"a\":2}. The check, however, gives the same answer for both: its _FENCE regex only matches fences tagged json or JSON or with no tag, so it does not see the ```text fence, and it scores the later top-level object {\"a\":2} in Reply A, exactly as it scores Reply B. I would be wrong if recover_json_object on Reply A actually returns {\"a\":1}, which would show the code does honor non-JSON fences; I would also be wrong if the documented rule is read as applying only to fences tagged json or JSON, because then the rule would require the same answer for both replies and this pair would not be a required difference."
